This is an evidence-quality bug worth fixing: the classification claims "file:line provenance resolved from source". Let me fix `adapter_provenance` to:
- derive the *module path* from the qualified name and map to a file: for `UpstreamAdapters.DownstreamUse.stdFormR`, the module is `UpstreamAdapters.DownstreamUse` → file `adapters/UpstreamAdapters/DownstreamUse.lean`; for `UpstreamAdaptersPetersen.DownstreamUse.Plane` → `adapters/UpstreamAdaptersPetersen/DownstreamUse.lean`. But some entries are in `UpstreamAdapters.DownstreamGeometry` etc. So: try to find the file whose module path matches the longest prefix of the name; then search that file for the short name; fall back to the global scan.
- support declaration modifiers in the regex.

Implementation:
```python
DECL_RX = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*(?:@\[[^\]]*\][ \t]*)?(?:private[ \t]+|protected[ \t]+|noncomputable[ \t]+|partial[ \t]+|scoped[ \t]+|unsafe[ \t]+)*(?:theorem|lemma|def|abbrev|opaque|alias)[ \t]+" + short + r"\b", re.M)
```
Plus module-path preference:
```python
def adapter_provenance(root, name):
    short = name.rsplit(".",1)[1]
    prefix = name.rsplit(".",1)[0]  # e.g. UpstreamAdapters.DownstreamUse
    files = sorted(p for p in (root/"adapters").rglob("*.lean") if ".lake" not in p.parts)
    def module_of(p): return ".".join(p.relative_to(root/"adapters").with_suffix("").parts)
    preferred = [p for p in files if module_of(p) == prefix]
    ordered = preferred + [p for p in files if p not in preferred]
    for p in ordered:
        ...
```
